Specifically, starting from any node $i$ and following the edges $i \to A_i \to A_{A_i} \dots$, we will eventually enter a cycle and stay there.
The condition $x_i \leq x_{A_i}$ implies that if we traverse an edge $u \to v$, then $x_u \leq x_v$.
If there is a cycle in the graph, say $c_1 \to c_2 \to \dots \to c_k \to c_1$, then we must have $x_{c_1} \leq x_{c_2} \leq \dots \leq x_{c_k} \leq x_{c_1}$. This implies $x_{c_1} = x_{c_2} = \dots = x_{c_k}$.
So, for any cycle, all nodes in the cycle must have the same value.
Furthermore, for any node $u$ that eventually leads to a cycle, say $u \to v \to \dots \to \text{cycle}$, we have $x_u \leq x_v \leq \dots$.
Actually, the condition is $x_i \leq x_{A_i}$. So if $i$ points to $A_i$, then $x_i \leq x_{A_i}$.
Let's rephrase: $x_u \leq x_v$ if there is an edge $u \to v$.
If there is a cycle, all nodes in the cycle must have the same value. Let the value of the cycle be $v_c$.
Then for any node $u$ that leads to the cycle, $x_u \leq x_{\text{next}(u)} \leq \dots \leq v_c$.
So $x_u$ is bounded above by the value of the cycle it leads to.
Wait, this interpretation assumes the edges are $i \to A_i$. Let's check the sample cases.
