If we have a very small $x$, it is easy to find a $y$ such that $x \le y/2$.
If we have a very large $y$, it is easy to find an $x$ such that $x \le y/2$.
If we have a very small $y$, it is hard to find an $x$ such that $x \le y/2$.
If we have a very large $x$, it is hard to find a $y$ such that $x \le y/2$.

So, small elements are "good" top mochi candidates (easy to satisfy condition) and "bad" bottom mochi candidates (hard to satisfy condition).
Large elements are "bad" top mochi candidates (hard to satisfy condition) and "good" bottom mochi candidates (easy to satisfy condition).

